# Redeploy After Commit (Koyeb & Cloudflare)

When planning or completing work that touches the **backend** or **database**, or the **web** app, ensure the right deployment step runs so the live site is updated.

**Agent responsibility:** When the user asks to push, go live, or deploy after a commit, **run** push and redeploy commands yourself. Do not only tell the user what to run unless a command fails (e.g. missing Koyeb login).

## Koyeb (backend + DB)

- **Service:** `database/vettr` (app-name/service-name).
- **When:** Any commit that changes `backend/` or that requires DB migrations (e.g. after changing `backend/src/db/migrate.js`).
- **Redeploy command (run from project root):**
  ```bash
  koyeb services redeploy database/vettr
  ```
- **Prereqs:** [Koyeb CLI](https://github.com/koyeb/koyeb-cli) installed and logged in (`koyeb login --token YOUR_API_TOKEN`). Config in `~/.koyeb.yaml`.
- **After backend redeploy:** If you added a migration, run migrations on the Koyeb DB (e.g. via Koyeb shell or one-off run: `npm run migrate`).

## Cloudflare Pages (web)

- **When:** Any commit that changes `web/` and is pushed to the branch connected to Cloudflare (usually `main`).
- **Default:** Cloudflare Pages is connected to the GitHub repo; pushing to `main` triggers an automatic build and deploy. No extra step if you push.
- **If auto-deploy is off or you need a manual deploy:** Cloudflare dashboard → your Pages project → Deployments → “Retry deployment” or “Create deployment” from the branch.

## Workflow after commit

1. **Backend-only or DB change:** Commit, **push**, then run `koyeb services redeploy database/vettr` (and run migrations on Koyeb if needed).
2. **Web-only change:** Commit and **push** to `main`; Cloudflare will deploy if connected.
3. **Backend and web change:** Commit, **push**, run `koyeb services redeploy database/vettr`; Cloudflare will deploy from the push.

When completing a commit that affects backend or DB, **run** the Koyeb redeploy command (report success or the exact error if CLI is not logged in). When the commit affects `web/`, confirm push completed and Cloudflare will build from `main`.
